"An API resource diagram describes client-visible resources, supported operations, and navigable relationships without exposing internal storage.\n\nUse when: Review a public API vocabulary and its discoverable links. Explain resource ownership and action placement before specifying endpoints.\n\nAvoid when: Internal table design or exact request timing is the real question.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes name resources and optionally show representative URI templates. Verb labels identify supported operations on the owning resource. Directed links denote navigation or operation links, distinct from database foreign keys.\n\nBuild: Start from client tasks and identify stable resource identities. Attach operations to the resource whose behavior they express. Add meaningful relationships and show collection versus item boundaries. Keep URI examples short, pad resource cards, and route link labels outside operation lists.\n\nCheck: Every operation has a defined target and documented meaning. URI nesting is not mistaken for security or ownership enforcement. Planned endpoints are distinguished from the current contract. Relationship arrows do not cross resource names or operation text.\n\nPitfalls: Copying database tables directly into the external resource vocabulary. Using unlabeled arrows that could mean containment, navigation, or invocation.\n\nExample: Orders exposes create and retrieve operations, while an Order links to its Payments collection.\n\nAlternatives: Entity relationship diagram, Sequence diagram."
